s applies? But then \"first insurance\" seems tautological. The spec also says \"Customers receive a 15% discount on each contract after their first.\" So there's the distinction between a first contract and subsequent ones.\n\nAnd the clarifying question: \"does first insurance mean the customer's first ever contract, or the first time we see this particular item?\" -> The answer: \"The first insurance surcharge still applies to the new sword, even though the customer is on a follow-up contract — each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer history.\"\n\nWait, but that says first insurance applies to every item always? That would mean every quote always has +10%. But then why have the clarifying answer? Let me re-read the integration example:\n\n\"Long-standing customer's second contract: 3 years, cursed sword enchantment 7: 100 G base + 50 G curse + 30 G high enchantment − 20 G loyalty + 10 G first insurance − 15 G follow-up contract = 155 G + 5 G fee = 160 G\"\n\nThere's both +10% first insurance AND -15% follow-up contract. So first insurance always applies (to the items in any quote) AND follow-up also applies (to quotes after the first).\n\nThat means my original test expectations were wrong. I need to update them. But the current tests were written in the test list based on the spec examples... Let me reconsider.\n\nActually wait: I'm confusing myself. Let me look at the specific test expectations I wrote. My test \"should return premium 105 G for a single sword (100 G base + 5 G fee)\" had no first insurance in it. But now I've learned first insurance always applies. So the correct value should be 115 G (100 base + 10% = 110 + 5 fee = 115). \n\nBut that contradicts the test description \"105 G for a single sword (100 G base + 5 G fee)\". The integration example \"First thing elsewhere\" says newcomers get 10%. \n\nHmm, but looking at the integration example for the \"newcomer with cursed sword\": \"100 G base + 50 G curse + 10 G first insurance = 160 G + 5 G fee = 165 G\". The first insurance is there.\n\nSo my earlier tests were wrong. Every quote gets first insurance surcharge. Let me update all the existing test expectations. But that also means every test should be revised...\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-think: the test list is what I wrote. I can update the test expectations now that I understand the spec better. Let me fix all the tests to include first insurance +10% and fix the rounding issue (need to round to whole G and round up for premiums).\n\nI need to:\n1. Add rounding to the implementation (round up for premiums)\n2. Update all test expectations to include first insurance\n\nLet me first update the existing tests that need correcting, then fix the implementation.\n\n\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_cfac60bb4bd34a38b1151130","name":"edit","arguments":{"edits":[{"newText":"  it(\"should return premium 115 G for a single sword (100 G base + 10 G first insurance + 5 G fee)\", () => {\n    const input = {\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [{ op: \"quote\", items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false }] }],\n    };\n    const result = processScenario(input);\n    expect(result).toEqual({ results: [{ premium: 115 }] });\n  });","oldText":"  it(\"should return premium 105 G for a single sword (100 G base + 5 G fee)\", () => {\n    const input = {\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [{ op: \"quote\", items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false }] }],\n    };\n    const result = processScenario(input);\n    expect(result).toEqual({ results: [{ premium: 105 }] });\n  });"},{"newText":"  it(\"should return premium 71 G for a single amulet (60 G base + 6 G first insurance + 5 G fee)\", () => {\n    const input = {\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [{ op: \"quote\", items: [{ type: \"amulet\", material: \"silver\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false }] }],\n    }